value(RObject) -- convert R object to Macaulay2
***********************************************

  * Function: *note value: (Macaulay2Doc)value,
  * Usage: 
        value x
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note R object: RObject,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note thing: (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, the Macaulay2 equivalent of x; 

Description
===========

If possible, the Macaulay2 equivalent of the given *note RObject: RObject, is
returned.

Note that most R objects are vectors.  When a vector has length 1, the
corresponding Macaulay2 object is returned as a scalar.

When the input is R's NULL, *note null: (Macaulay2Doc)null, is returned.

+-------------------------+
|i1 : x = RObject null    |
|                         |
|o1 = NULL                |
|                         |
|o1 : RObject of type NULL|
+-------------------------+
|i2 : value x === null    |
|                         |
|o2 = true                |
+-------------------------+

When the input is a logical vector, a *note Boolean: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean, is
returned.

+----------------------------+
|i3 : x = RObject true       |
|                            |
|o3 = [1] TRUE               |
|                            |
|o3 : RObject of type logical|
+----------------------------+
|i4 : value x                |
|                            |
|o4 = true                   |
+----------------------------+

When the input is an integer vector, a *note ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ, object is
returned.

+----------------------------+
|i5 : x = RObject 5          |
|                            |
|o5 = [1] 5                  |
|                            |
|o5 : RObject of type integer|
+----------------------------+
|i6 : value x                |
|                            |
|o6 = 5                      |
+----------------------------+

When the input is a double vector, a *note RR: (Macaulay2Doc)RR, object is
returned.

+---------------------------+
|i7 : x = RObject (3/2)     |
|                           |
|o7 = [1] 1.5               |
|                           |
|o7 : RObject of type double|
+---------------------------+
|i8 : value x               |
|                           |
|o8 = 1.5                   |
|                           |
|o8 : RR (of precision 53)  |
+---------------------------+

When the input is a complex vector, a *note CC: (Macaulay2Doc)CC, object is
returned.

+----------------------------+
|i9 : x = RObject ii         |
|                            |
|o9 = [1] 0+1i               |
|                            |
|o9 : RObject of type complex|
+----------------------------+
|i10 : value x               |
|                            |
|o10 = ii                    |
|                            |
|o10 : CC (of precision 53)  |
+----------------------------+

When the input is a character vector, a *note String: (Macaulay2Doc)String,
object is returned.

+-------------------------------+
|i11 : x = RObject foo          |
|                               |
|o11 = [1] "foo"                |
|                               |
|o11 : RObject of type character|
+-------------------------------+
|i12 : value x                  |
|                               |
|o12 = foo                      |
+-------------------------------+

When the input is a vector with more than one element, a *note List:
(Macaulay2Doc)List, object is returned.

+------------------------------+
|i13 : x = RObject {1, 3, 5, 7}|
|                              |
|o13 = [1] 1 3 5 7             |
|                              |
|o13 : RObject of type integer |
+------------------------------+
|i14 : value x                 |
|                              |
|o14 = {1, 3, 5, 7}            |
|                              |
|o14 : List                    |
+------------------------------+

When the input is a pairlist (R's linked list type), a *note Sequence:
(Macaulay2Doc)Sequence, is returned.

+------------------------------+
|i15 : x = RObject (3, 6, 9)   |
|                              |
|o15 = [[1]]                   |
|      [1] 3                   |
|                              |
|      [[2]]                   |
|      [1] 6                   |
|                              |
|      [[3]]                   |
|      [1] 9                   |
|                              |
|                              |
|o15 : RObject of type pairlist|
+------------------------------+
|i16 : value x                 |
|                              |
|o16 = (3, 6, 9)               |
|                              |
|o16 : Sequence                |
+------------------------------+

There also exists a list type in R, created by the function list, that may
contain elements of of any type, much like Macaulay2 lists.  When the input is
such a list, a *note List: (Macaulay2Doc)List, object is returned.

+------------------------------+
|i17 : RList = RFunction "list"|
|                              |
|o17 = RList                   |
|                              |
|o17 : RFunction               |
+------------------------------+
|i18 : x = RList(2, 4, 6, 8)   |
|                              |
|o18 = [[1]]                   |
|      [1] 2                   |
|                              |
|      [[2]]                   |
|      [1] 4                   |
|                              |
|      [[3]]                   |
|      [1] 6                   |
|                              |
|      [[4]]                   |
|      [1] 8                   |
|                              |
|                              |
|o18 : RObject of type list    |
+------------------------------+
|i19 : value x                 |
|                              |
|o19 = {2, 4, 6, 8}            |
|                              |
|o19 : List                    |
+------------------------------+

When the input is a matrix or array, a nested *note List: (Macaulay2Doc)List,
object is returned.  Note that R uses column-major order for matrices, unlike
Macaulay2, which uses row-major order.  No attempt is made to change the order,
unlike *note new RObject from Matrix: new RObject from Matrix,, which does do
this conversion.  For the most part, value and *note RObject: RObject, are
inverses of one another, but this is an exception.

+------------------------------+
|i20 : A = random(ZZ^2, ZZ^3)  |
|                              |
|o20 = | 8 3 8 |               |
|      | 1 7 3 |               |
|                              |
|               2       3      |
|o20 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ       |
+------------------------------+
|i21 : x = RObject A           |
|                              |
|o21 =      [,1] [,2] [,3]     |
|      [1,]    8    3    8     |
|      [2,]    1    7    3     |
|                              |
|o21 : RObject of type integer |
+------------------------------+
|i22 : value x                 |
|                              |
|o22 = {{8, 1}, {3, 7}, {8, 3}}|
|                              |
|o22 : List                    |
+------------------------------+
|i23 : A == transpose matrix oo|
|                              |
|o23 = true                    |
+------------------------------+

When the input has any names, the elements with names are returned as *note
Option: (Macaulay2Doc)Option, objects.

+-----------------------------------------+
|i24 : x = RObject {foo => 1, 2, bar => 3}|
|                                         |
|o24 = foo     bar                        |
|        1   2   3                        |
|                                         |
|o24 : RObject of type integer            |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i25 : value x                            |
|                                         |
|o25 = {foo => 1, 2, bar => 3}            |
|                                         |
|o25 : List                               |
+-----------------------------------------+

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note value(RObject): value_lpRObject_rp, -- convert R object to Macaulay2
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in RInterface/doc/value.m2:90:0.

